[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"EF Core Change Tracker and Unit of Work Pattern in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Entity Framework (EF) Core provides a powerful mechanism called the Change Tracker to monitor the state of your entities. When combined with the Unit of Work pattern, you can manage database transactions cleanly, ensuring that multiple repository operations execute within a single atomic scope."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the EF Core Change Tracker"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Change Tracker keeps track of every entity instance loaded from the database context. It assigns an entity state to each tracked object, determining how EF Core will generate SQL commands during the SaveChanges call."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Added: The entity is new and needs to be inserted into the database."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Modified: The entity exists and its property values have changed."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deleted: The entity is marked for removal from the database."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unchanged: The entity was queried, but no properties have been modified."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The Unit of Work Pattern"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Unit of Work pattern maintains a list of objects affected by a business transaction and coordinates the writing out of changes. In EF Core, the DbContext itself acts as the Unit of Work, and the Change Tracker serves as the registry of business objects."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Custom Unit of Work Interface"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"While DbContext is already a Unit of Work, wrapping it in a custom interface helps abstract persistence details and improves testability."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public interface IUnitOfWork : IDisposable
{
    IRepository<TEntity> Repository<TEntity>() where TEntity : class;
    Task<int> CompleteAsync();
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing the Unit of Work Class"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The implementation manages the DbContext instance and caches repositories to ensure they share the same tracking context."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class UnitOfWork : IUnitOfWork
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _context;
    private Dictionary<string, object> _repositories;

    public UnitOfWork(AppDbContext context)
    {
        _context = context;
    }

    public IRepository<TEntity> Repository<TEntity>() where TEntity : class
    {
        _repositories ??= new Dictionary<string, object>();

        var type = typeof(TEntity).Name;

        if (!_repositories.ContainsKey(type))
        {
            var repositoryInstance = Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(Repository<TEntity>), _context);
            _repositories.Add(type, repositoryInstance);
        }

        return (IRepository<TEntity>)_repositories[type];
    }

    public async Task<int> CompleteAsync()
    {
        return await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _context.Dispose();
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Leveraging Change Tracker in Repositories"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because the repositories share the DbContext, updates are implicitly managed by the Change Tracker without needing explicit update statements."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"public class Repository<TEntity> : IRepository<TEntity> where TEntity : class
{
    protected readonly AppDbContext _context;

    public Repository(AppDbContext context)
    {
        _context = context;
    }

    public async Task<TEntity> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Set<TEntity>().FindAsync(id);
    }

    public void Update(TEntity entity)
    {
        _context.Set<TEntity>().Attach(entity);
        _context.Entry(entity).State = EntityState.Modified;
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"EF Core's Change Tracker combined with the Unit of Work pattern provides an efficient architecture for handling enterprise data operations. It minimizes database roundtrips, ensures transaction integrity, and simplifies application logic."}]
